{
  "abstract": "We classify a finite but structurally broad family of possible order-21 ten-chromatic obstructions to the Earth–Moon problem. Let G be K9-free with at most 114 edges, and suppose four independent vertices of degree at least nine leave a spanning clique blow-up of C5 on seventeen vertices, with every bag of size at least two. We enumerate all thirteen positive core vectors, all edge-budget-permitted one- and two-edge core augmentations, and every labeled outsider-neighborhood multiset in the cap-feasible degree patterns. Core nine-colorings are enumerated as singleton-plus-eight-pair matchings, and outsider neighborhoods become exact blocker bitsets. The complete search gives 73 graph isomorphism classes of non-nine-colorable attachments. Every class has an independently checked linking certificate proving non-biplanarity; six terminal classes under explicit edge-deletion maps are ten-critical, with direct nine-colorings for every edge deletion. Four terminal classes match published order-21 examples, while two are new relative to that list. Therefore every biplanar graph in this spanning-core family is nine-colorable. The result combines a complete attachment classification with a reusable bridge from exact coloring covers to geometric non-biplanarity. It does not claim a census of all order-21 critical graphs or resolve the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem. Code, literal edge maps, certificates, and reproduction instructions are supplied in a compact supplement.",
